# This module's Go sources are LF in the index and must be LF in the worktree
# too. gofmt compares the bytes ON DISK, so a CRLF checkout makes `gofmt -l`
# list a file that is byte-identical to a clean one — measured: main.go's entire
# gofmt diff was 578 lines of ^M and nothing else. eol=lf overrides
# core.autocrlf=true, which is what put the CRLF there in the first place.
*.go text eol=lf

# The committed CLI binary has no file extension, so git classifies it by
# content sniffing alone. This makes that explicit and immune to any future
# broad rule such as `* text=auto`: the first 12 digits of this file's sha256
# are the clihash segment of every production cache key, and a single converted
# byte would both corrupt the ELF and silently re-key the cache. `binary` is the
# -text -diff -merge macro; all three are correct for a 24MB build artefact.
#
# The hash is deliberately not quoted here any more. It was, and it went stale:
# the comment named b659fff65cb9 while the vendored binary had long since become
# e0c9ab3e2c51. A value that must be re-checked to be trusted is worse than no
# value, because a reader believes it. Measure it instead:
#   sha256sum bin/scientific-consensus-pp-cli-linux | cut -c1-12
bin/** binary

# The vendored CLI is tracked with Git LFS. git cannot delta-compress a 24 MB
# binary between versions, so every vendoring added another full copy to the
# history permanently. Measured on 2026-09-20: 40 distinct blobs, and the packed
# repository at 127 MiB after gc.
#
# LFS stores a ~130 byte pointer in the commit and keeps the binary outside the
# object database, so a clone fetches the current version instead of all forty.
#
# This line comes AFTER `bin/** binary` on purpose: the last matching rule wins,
# and the LFS filter must be the one that applies. -text is repeated here rather
# than inherited, so this line remains correct on its own if the rule above ever
# moves or is removed.
#
# This stops future growth only. The forty blobs already in the history stay
# where they are: removing them means rewriting history, which invalidates every
# existing clone and every commit hash. That is a separate decision.
#
# Anyone checking out this repository needs git-lfs installed. CI already checks
# out with lfs: true in both jobs.
bin/scientific-consensus-pp-cli-linux filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text